  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. First, we determine which outcomes are 'probable' according to the criterion: an outcome is probable if its probability is greater than 1 divided by the number of outcomes in that lottery. Lottery A has 3 outcomes (4000€, 2000€, 0€), so the threshold is 1/3 ≈ 33.3%. In Lottery A, only the 0€ outcome (50%) exceeds this threshold. The average of probable outcomes in A is thus 0€. Lottery B has 2 outcomes (6000€, 0€), so the threshold is 1/2 = 50%. In Lottery B, the 0€ outcome has 75%, which exceeds 50%, while 6000€ has 25%, which does not. Thus, only 0€ is considered probable in B, and the average of probable outcomes is also 0€. Since both lotteries have the same average (0€) over their probable outcomes, the decision reason would be indifferent between A and B.",
